            <ArticleTitle>A rare case of splenic infarct presenting with acute abdominal pain due to polyarteritis nodosa: case report and review of the literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>242-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 44 year-old man presented to the Emergency Department of American Hospital with severe pain persisting in the left upper quadrant for several hours. A computed tomographic scanning (CT scan) and celiac digital substraction angiography (DSA) of the abdomen demonstrated a splenic infarct. Histopathological examinations showed diffuse acute vasculitis, thrombosis, panvasculitis which led us to diagnose the case as &quot;polyarteritis nodosa&quot;. The diagnosis is usually difficult to establish and it is usually delayed due to variable clinical manifestations dependent on the site and the extent of arterial involvement.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
